Play! (https://github.com/jpd002/Play-, BSD licence, Copyright (c) 2006-2026 Jean-Philip Desjardins) runs the
PlayStation 2 on a thread of its own, a frame or two ahead of its libretro frontend. The tools apply this change of
Dreamports' own to its source before building it (tools/consoles.mjs, tools/libretro/build.mjs), so that each retro_run
is one frame of the console: the console waits at each V-blank, before it reads the buttons, until the frontend asks
for the next frame, the frontend serves the GS's calls until it has got there, and every block of sound is kept and
handed over with the frame it was mixed in, 45 samples at a time (docs/CONSOLES.md, The PlayStation 2).

It also keeps each frame's draw list: a hash of everything the console gave the GS that decides its picture, in the order
given, every register write, every picture sent and what the screen shows of the framebuffer, which the frontend asks for
with dreamports_draw_list. That's what a 3D game is proven by (docs/STANDARD.md).

